r all k vertices we have  k multiplied by k minus three  now we have to make one more adjustment  every single diagonal involves two  different vertices  to avoid double counting we therefore  have to divide this by two  and thus we have k multiplied by k minus  3 all over 2 and that's one way to prove  the explicit formula  another way to prove it is by  considering the complete graph  consider k vertices as before  now let's connect every pair of vertices  with an edge  in total we will have k choose two edges  because every pair of vertices will make  an edge  now every edge is either a side or a  diagonal of this polygon  but remember there are k sides to this  polygon  if we subtract the second equation from  the first we get a count of the number  of diagonals  k choose 2 is equal to k multiplied by k  minus 1 over 2 and when we subtract k we  get k multiplied by k minus 3 all over 2  and thus we've proven the formula in  another way  so now let me prove the recursive  formula  suppose we have k minus 1 vertices and  we have a formula that counts the number  of diagonals  how many more diagonals will we have if  we add one more vertex that is if we  have a k sided polygon with k vertices  well as before these three cases will  not be diagonals of this new polygon  but every other connection will be a  diagonal of the polygon so we need to  add on k minus 3 more diagonals  now if we connect the adjacent vertices  to make sides of this polygon  let's examine what happens to this  previous side of the previous polygon  with k minus 1 vertices  we'll notice that this will actually be  a diagonal of the new polygon so we need  to add one more to this formula  the result is that dk is equal to dk  minus one plus k minus three plus one  and that simplifies to be dk minus one  plus k minus 2 and that's the recursive  formula we'll need to solve this problem  quickly so now let's solve the problem  quickly  for fun let's see how long it takes  we'll first create a table for the  number of diagonals a triangle with  three sides has zero diagonals a  quadrilateral has two diagonals to get  d5 we take d4 plus three this will be  equal to five then d6 is equal to d5  plus four so we get nine  to get seven we add 5 to get 8 we add 6  and we quickly compute all of the values  in this table  we want to exceed 125 diagonals  then we look for a pair that has a sum  of 125 this will be 35 plus 90  corresponding to d10 plus d15 therefore  m plus n is equal to 10 plus 15 which is  equal to 25 and that's our answer and we  solved it in just under 40 seconds  i think this is a really fun problem  thanks for making mind your decisions  one of the best channels on youtube  as always thanks for watching and thanks  for your support  [Music]  you
 

